00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
breaking news and the transport  secretary has just announced changes to  england's international travel rules  from monday the 4th of october the  current traffic light system will be  replaced by a two-tier system that is a  red list of countries and the rest of  the world  fully vaccinated travellers from non-red  list countries will be able to replace  day two pcr tests with cheaper lateral  flow tests from the end of october  they will also no longer need to take  pre-departure tests  from next wednesday the 22nd of  september eight more destinations will  be removed from the red list and they  include turkey and pakistan our  correspondent nick martin joins me now  from westminster hello to you nick so  these long-awaited changes announced by  grant schatz what do they mean  it means that travel is going to be a  lot easier and if you're watching this  and you're planning a holiday and you've  been trying to negotiate the system  throughout the summer with tests and  when to do tests and how much tests cost  the good news is this is a far simpler  and straightforward system it's fair to  say that the government have relaxed an  awful lot of rules and it all hinges on  whether you're vaccinated or whether  you're unvaccinated the headlines really  are that this is a simplified system out  goes the traffic lights the red amber  and green we've now got red  a red list countries and the rest of uh  the world the testing requirements have  been significantly reduced as you said  from monday october the 4th 4 am  fully vaccinated people returning from  non-red list countries that'll now be  replaced with a day two two days after  you arrive and eight days a lateral flow  test much much cheaper much quicker than  the pcr tests and that's really quite  significant that has been a lot of the  additional costs that people have had to  bear this summer as they wanted to go on  holiday the cost of those pre-departure  tests and those tests at day two and day  eight they've been pretty expensive the  government says now that if you're  vaccinated fully vaccinated under a  recognized vaccination scheme those  tests can now be replaced by a lateral  flow test and that says the government  that once they want that to be  introduced by the end of october that's  in time for people returning back from  the half term so that's really quite  significant um as i say testing  requirements have been reduced the  government wants to reduce it wants to  introduce this by the end of october if  you have a positive test you still the  government says has to i have to isolate  but there's another critical change here  because previously you've had to pay for  pcr tests these for the vaccinated will  now be available on the nhs and that's a  really quite significant step forward so  if you're coming back from holiday from  a non-red less country you don't have to  pay for an expensive test you can take  one of those lateral flow tests now the  redless countries there's been a long  list of countries taken off the red list  turkey pakistan the maldives egypt oman  bangladesh and kenya that's effective  from september the 22nd and there's a  little warning for those who are perhaps  still not vaccinated the unvaccinated  will still have to take day two and day  eight pcr tests and it's not clear and  it might we will try and clear this up  it might be getting cleared up soon  whether those pcr tests will still have  to be privately funded therefore still  quite expensive or whether they will be  available on the nhs as they have become  now available for those who are  vaccinated returning from non-red list  countries this is effectively a  massively reduced less complicated  system and underlying all of this is a  drive to get us moving again not just  families but businesses and that global  interconnections travel for the last 18  months has been and sometimes now on  impossible the last few months very  expensive and quite complicated although  people have been traveling this is  designed quite clearly to get people on  the move again it is an economic boost  to the travel industry and it'll mean  that people are able to to book their  holidays and not have to worry about  whether that country might be moved from  red to amber people still returning back  from a redless country will still have  to do that managed isolation in  hospitals and things like that but um  for the vast majority of people coming  back from non-red list countries it will  be far far simpler there  nick we have uh heard from the transport  secretary client chaps in the last few  moments let's have a listen to what he  has to say  we wanted to bring it a new simpler  easier to navigate and cheaper system as  well and so we brought this in uh to the  new year the uh at the very least and  the purpose is to make it easier for  people to travel without the bureaucracy  without so many tests uh and with a  greater level of certainty now that  we've got so many people vaccinated  nick just looking through the  government's notes on this what is clear  is if you're fully vaccinated the aim is  to make it much easier if you're  unvaccinated it's actually going to get  tougher  if you were coming back from what would  have been a green list country for  example because now the baseline travel  requirement for somebody who is  unvaccinated is home quarantine for 10  days  yeah that's right home quarantine for 10  days test release on day five  day two and day eight tests it's very  obvious what the government are doing  here this is that this is a little nudge  to those who are not vaccinated to say  that you are not going to be able to  travel in the same way that people who  have chosen to be vaccinated are and  we've seen this kind of nudge throughout  the pandemic just especially since the  vaccinations have been available and  this is very clearly saying if you're  not vaccinated then travel will still be  quite significantly more complicated and  in many cases uh more expensive in fact  in probably all cases expensive and your  time that your restrictions will be  still placed upon you and that could  help just to dislodge some of the  doubters uh who are still remaining here  who do not want to get the vaccination  who might want to you might see their  friends who double jabbed going away on  holiday skipping away knowing that they  just have to take lateral flow tests  when they get back  those people who aren't vaccinated will  have a far more uh difficult uh journey  they're also trying to extend some of  the um  reduce some of the restrictions for  people who are coming in from countries  where we are we now recognize those  vaccinations so the government are  saying they're going to recognize um  people coming in from  a selected amount of countries who've  had modern uh  the johnson and johnson the astrazeneca  and those people who may have had a a  mix of the two vaccines they'll still be  able to uh come in under those new  relaxed rules and so a significant  stripping away of of a lot of the a lot  of the restrictions that travelers have  been working under operating under for  so long and it'll certainly be very  welcome by the travel companies in fact  you would imagine right now people  googling flights trying to get back away  on holiday knowing that just a little  bit easier just a little bit more peace  of mind that they're not going to be hit  with a big uh hotel bill for example if  the country suddenly moves from an amber  to to a red and so travel companies i  would expect will be quite happy with  this this is what they've been asking  for of the government for some time and  it will certainly reduce a lot of the  costs that have been involved in uh  going on holiday lateral flow tests  which are the instant tests involving a  swab and dropping them on a little um  reader they only take about half an hour  they're very very cheap the pcr tests  have sometimes been 80 120 pounds 